    {
      "path": "internal/syncschema/schema_test.go",
      "content": "With the addition of KindLLMProvider and KindMCPServer, the legacy TestValidateIdentityAcceptsFiveKinds test name now understates the count (it covers 5; the two new kinds are covered by TestValidateIdentityAcceptsConfigSourceKinds). Consider renaming to TestValidateIdentityAcceptsLegacyFiveKinds or merging all accepted-kind cases so the test list stays authoritative — non-blocking, current behavior is correct.",
      "existing_code": "func TestValidateIdentityAcceptsFiveKinds(t *testing.T) {",
      "start_line": 0,
      "end_line": 0,
      "category": "style",
      "severity": "low",
      "priority": "P3"
    },
    {
      "path": "internal/env/manager.go",
      "content": "The `errors.Is(err, os.ErrNotExist)` contract added here is only meaningful if `Delete` and `RenameKey` (and the in-memory \"not found\" path at manager.go:204 and manager.go:513) apply the same wrapping. Right now they still return `fmt.Errorf(\"variable %s not found in group %s\", key, group)` without `%w`, so callers cannot rely on `errors.Is(err, os.ErrNotExist)` uniformly across the Manager API — only `Get` exposes it. Consider extending the wrapping to all \"not found\" sites in this file for consistency, or document the asymmetry in the package comment.",
      "suggestion_code": "// 保留哨兵错误（%w），调用方可用 errors.Is(err, os.ErrNotExist) 判定\"条目缺失\"\nreturn \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"variable %s not found in group %s: %w\", key, group, err)",
      "existing_code": "// 保留哨兵错误（%w），调用方可用 errors.Is 判定\"条目缺失\"\nreturn \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"variable %s not found in group %s: %w\", key, group, err)",
      "start_line": 120,
      "end_line": 121,
      "category": "maintainability",
      "severity": "low",
      "priority": "P3"
    },
    {
      "path": "internal/mcp/export.go",
      "content": "executeTarget re-resolves entries (lines 295, 329) and discards the freshly-generated warnings, so the final `item.Warnings` only reflects Plan-time state. For env-template resolution this is currently benign (deterministic), but it creates a silent invariant: if `ResolveLoose` ever becomes stateful or the stored entry changes between Plan and executeTarget, the user-visible warnings would not match what was actually written. Consider either reusing the Plan-time resolved server (e.g., attach it to the item or stash it on a shared map) or updating `item.Warnings` from the second resolve call.",
      "existing_code": "\t\tserver, _, err := e.resolveEntry(entry)\n\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\titem.Action = ActionError\n\t\t\titem.Reason = err.Error()\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tfile.set(item.Alias, server)",
      "start_line": 295,
      "end_line": 301,
      "category": "maintainability",
      "severity": "low",
      "priority": "P2"
    },
    {
      "path": "internal/mcp/export.go",
      "content": "When `ResolveLoose` returns multiple unresolved references for a single field, the current `fmt.Sprintf(\"...: %s\", warn)` formats the `[]string` with Go's default `[a b]` slice syntax, e.g. `MCP server \"x\" env FOO: [missing env BAR missing env BAZ]`. Emit one warning per unresolved reference (or join with `, `) so each entry is independently readable and greppable.",
      "existing_code": "\t\t\tif len(warn) > 0 {\n\t\t\t\twarnings = append(warnings, fmt.Sprintf(\"MCP server %q %s: %s\", entry.Alias, field, warn))\n\t\t\t}",
      "start_line": 362,
      "end_line": 364,
      "category": "maintainability",
      "severity": "low",
      "priority": "P1"
    },
    {
      "path": "internal/mcp/export.go",
      "content": "The `ResolveLoose` contract doc says it returns a warning instead of an error for unresolvable references, and the Plan comment hints at \"reference cycles etc.\" for hard errors, but the actual boundary (any non-nil `err` from `ResolveLoose` becomes a hard error that poisons the target) is not spelled out. Document the contract on `ResolveLoose` explicitly — e.g., \"the callback must return a non-nil error only for unresolvable *structural* conditions (cycles, malformed templates); missing env/text values must come back as `(raw, [warning], nil)`\" — so future implementers don't silently turn loose warnings into hard failures.",
      "existing_code": "// ResolveLoose, when set, takes precedence: an unresolvable reference keeps\n// its template literal in the written value and returns a warning instead of\n// an error (profiles sync across machines before their credentials do).",
      "start_line": 59,
      "end_line": 61,
      "category": "documentation",
      "severity": "low",
      "priority": "P3"
    },
    {
      "path": "cmd/mcp_export.go",
      "content": "The `combinedGetter` instantiation pattern is now duplicated between `cmd/text.go:351-352` and the new code in `mcpExporter` (this file). Since both call sites use the identical construction and pass it to `ref.ResolveWithWarnings`, consider exposing a thin helper (e.g., `resolveValueWithLoose(value, envMgr, textMgr)`) in `cmd/text.go` to consolidate. Not blocking.\n\nSource pattern in `cmd/text.go:351`:\n```go\ngetter := &combinedGetter{envManager: envMgr, textManager: textMgr}\nopts := ref.ResolveOptions{Loose: loose, CurrentGroup: currentGroup}\nresult, warnings, err := ref.ResolveWithWarnings(value, getter, opts)\n```",
      "suggestion_code": "resolveLoose := func(value string) (string, []string, error) {\n\t\treturn resolveValueWith(value, true, \"\", envMgr, textMgr)\n\t}",
      "existing_code": "getter := &combinedGetter{envManager: envMgr, textManager: textMgr}\n\tresolveLoose := func(value string) (string, []string, error) {\n\t\treturn ref.ResolveWithWarnings(value, getter, ref.ResolveOptions{Loose: true})\n\t}",
      "start_line": 82,
      "end_line": 85,
      "category": "maintainability",
      "severity": "low",
      "priority": "P3"
    },
    {
      "path": "cmd/mcp_export.go",
      "content": "Minor UX consistency: between `printExportPlan` (which surfaces `[未解析引用]` markers) and `printExportSnippets` (the `--print` path), the snippets don't carry any visual marker for items that still contain template literals. Users reading only the snippet output might miss the unresolved-ref status. The stderr warning already covers this, but consider adding a comment line for affected items so the snippet is self-describing.\n\nThe `# %s — add to %s` header is the natural place; e.g. `# warning: ...` lines before each affected snippet.",
      "suggestion_code": "func printExportSnippets(plan *mcp.ExportPlan) {\n\tfor _, item := range plan.Items {\n\t\tserver := item.Server()\n\t\tif item.Action != mcp.ActionCreate && item.Action != mcp.ActionUpdate {\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\ttarget, ok := agentcfg.Find(item.Agent)\n\t\tif !ok {\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tfmt.Printf(\"\\n# %s — add to %s\\n\", item.AgentName, item.Path)\n\t\tfor _, warning := range item.Warnings {\n\t\t\tfmt.Printf(\"# ⚠ %s\\n\", warning)\n\t\t}",
      "existing_code": "func printExportSnippets(plan *mcp.ExportPlan) {\n\tfor _, item := range plan.Items {\n\t\tserver := item.Server()\n\t\tif item.Action != mcp.ActionCreate && item.Action != mcp.ActionUpdate {\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\ttarget, ok := agentcfg.Find(item.Agent)\n\t\tif !ok {\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tfmt.Printf(\"\\n# %s — add to %s\\n\", item.AgentName, item.Path)",
      "start_line": 259,
      "end_line": 269,
      "category": "maintainability",
      "severity": "low",
      "priority": "P3"
    },
    {
      "path": "internal/mcp/export.go",
      "content": "Warnings are aggregated once per alias (line 124-137) and then the same slice reference is assigned to every (target, alias) item on line 150. When the plan contains multiple targets (e.g. `--all`), `printExportItemWarnings` (cmd/mcp_export.go:251) prints the same warning once per target even though `ResolveLoose` is deterministic and the warning text already carries the alias (\"MCP server %q ...\"). For 5 targets and 2 unresolved fields the user gets 10 identical lines. Consider either (a) computing `resolveWarn` per alias and emitting it once outside the target loop, or (b) deduplicating in the printer (e.g. group by warning text and suffix the list of affected agents).",
      "existing_code": "\tresolveWarn := make(map[string][]string, len(names))\n\tfor _, alias := range names {\n\t\tentry, err := e.mgr.Get(alias)\n\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn nil, err\n\t\t}\n\t\tserver, warnings, err := e.resolveEntry(entry)\n\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\tresolveErr[alias] = err\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tdesired[alias] = server\n\t\tresolveWarn[alias] = warnings\n\t}",
      "start_line": 124,
      "end_line": 137,
      "category": "maintainability",
      "severity": "medium",
      "priority": "P1"
    },
    {
      "path": "internal/env/manager.go",
      "content": "Line 121 wraps the raw `securefs.PathError` (with `Path: \"env/<group>/<key>.enc\"`), so the user-visible error becomes `variable <KEY> not found in group <GROUP>: securefs read \"env/<group>/<key>.enc\": no such file or directory`. This leaks the internal storage layout convention in the user-facing message and is inconsistent with the cleaner `os.ErrNotExist` wrap at line 130. For parity and a less noisy user message, mirror line 130 and wrap `os.ErrNotExist` directly here — the `errors.Is(err, os.ErrNotExist)` contract is satisfied by either form, since `securefs.PathError` unwraps to `syscall.ENOENT` which implements `Is(os.ErrNotExist)`.",
      "suggestion_code": "if os.IsNotExist(err) {\n\t\t\treturn \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"variable %s not found in group %s: %w\", key, group, os.ErrNotExist)\n\t\t}",
      "existing_code": "if os.IsNotExist(err) {\n\t\t\t// 保留哨兵错误（%w），调用方可用 errors.Is 判定\"条目缺失\"\n\t\t\treturn \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"variable %s not found in group %s: %w\", key, group, err)\n\t\t}",
      "start_line": 119,
      "end_line": 122,
      "category": "maintainability",
      "severity": "low",
      "priority": "P2"
    }
